#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130036} :activity/id #uuid "b93dfde4-f3d0-56f7-90c3-7b5628613c8b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048800 :topic/id #uuid "c021e608-4c8b-5191-872f-f3e0b02d2d4a" :topic/math-academy-id 1983} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129173 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128992 :question/id #uuid "e17f982d-3d3e-5279-b507-d390e4f1970f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6116"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8537cc22-8d18-57c8-9ebf-921bd4d06ae7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048820}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366254}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Double Summation"} :step/id #uuid "ac75c745-5dbd-5824-9f7d-04a8faf6a86c" :step/math-academy-id 22705 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130377 :tutorial/content "Recall that we use **sigma notation** to write sums concisely. For example, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i\n$$\n\nrefers to the sum of all terms in the sequence $a_i,$ where the **index** $i$ ranges from $1$ to $n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i = a_1+a_2+\\cdots+a_n\n$$\n\nSigma notation can also represent sums whose terms depend on two indices. That is, **double sums** of the form \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^m \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $i$ ranges from $1$ to $m,$ and $j$ ranges from $1$ to $n\\mathbin.$ Thus, a double summation is just a \"sum of a sum.\" \n\nIn calculating double summations, we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we evaluate the inner sum by fixing the index of the outer sum ($i$) and incrementing only the inner index ($j$).\n\n- Next, we evaluate the outer sum by incrementing the outer sum index.\n\nTo illustrate how it works, suppose we are given the following double sum:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^2 \\sum_{j=1}^4 (i+j)\n$$\n\n \n\nTo compute this double sum, we can proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{4}(i+j) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}[({\\sum }_{j=1}^{4}(i+j))] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}[(i+1)+(i+2)+(i+3)+(i+4)] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}(4i+10) \\\\  & =4(1)+10+4(2)+10 \\\\  & =4+10+8+10 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLater, we will provide some formulas that will help us simplify many double summations calculations." :tutorial/id #uuid "3c842b98-cbdf-55a4-96a1-360697763430" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3867 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "583e493e-9206-508f-b782-7eec0883a7d5" :step/math-academy-id 22707 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130035}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130378 :tutorial/content "Double summations satisfy the following properties:\n\n**The Constant Multiple Rule** \n\nWe can factor out a constant multiple from a double summation:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n k a_{ij} = k\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}\n$$\n\n**The Sum Rule** \n\nWe can distribute a double summation over a sum of terms.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n (a_{ij} + b_{ij}) = \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} + \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n b_{ij}\n$$\n\n**The Double Sum of Units**\n\nRecall that for single summations, we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{j=1}^{n} 1 = \\underbrace{1+1+\\cdots+1}_{n\\textrm{ times}}  = n.\n$$\n\nAnalogously for double summations, we have that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n 1 = mn.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "41df0fcf-551a-58a5-b47e-ac1ddd121f7f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3868 :tutorial/title "The Sum and Constant Multiple Rules"} :step/id #uuid "3d0a00d2-9ccc-5261-bfa0-15e1bf5cb3c6" :step/math-academy-id 22708 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129174 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128993 :question/id #uuid "e8f3608f-68f2-5ba9-b618-447887e7f6fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6118"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4312c28-e298-5185-9009-e6ca243175fd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048922}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365122}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Sum and Constant Multiple Rules"} :step/id #uuid "42e12d98-e4bf-512d-b3c2-5c93584fc740" :step/math-academy-id 22710 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130196}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130195 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129259 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128835 :question/id #uuid "d180f745-028b-5859-8cad-4a49b5c20773" :question/math-academy-id "e-10521"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08375770-c610-5dae-b01b-c457de086cba" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371865}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule and Swap Rules"} :step/id #uuid "4e8c00d9-bc34-56d4-a71a-61e0e176e8fb" :step/math-academy-id 39727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130196 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130453 :tutorial/content "In addition, we have the following useful rules:\n\n**The Product Rule** \n\nWe can distribute a double summation over a product of *single-index* terms with *different* indices.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n  a_{i}b_j = \\sum_{i=1}^m a_i \\sum_{j=1}^n b_{j}\n$$\n\n**The Swap Rule** \n\nWe can swap the order of summations in a double summation.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}= \\sum_{j=1}^n\\sum_{i=1}^m a_{ij}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d25ceabe-e67a-59b3-9ed6-37be45644e5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6584 :tutorial/title "The Product and Swap Rules"} :step/id #uuid "5a28b905-cf2f-59e1-aaa2-6fb0064fcd4b" :step/math-academy-id 39728 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130195}}] :activity/title "Double Summations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129331}]}
